PCI backend request retirement
==============================

Run: bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/pciback_lifetime_test.sh

The fixture extracts the production request, deferred worker, reference release,
unlend and inbox-draining functions. It uses pthread locks and controlled
condition variables where the kernel uses locks and a workqueue. PCI config
access, reset, IRQ delivery and message decoding are modeled.

Four scenarios check:

* A queued request pins its original device grant. Unlend followed by a new grant
  with the same instance ID and BDF cannot redirect that request, or send its ACK
  into a later boot.
* A negative reply requires no config access and does not queue an ID lookup.
* Unlend revokes admission under the registry spinlock, then waits outside that
  lock for a slow config access and its reply before resetting the function.
* Direct and deferred config writes preserve the request value; single-function
  retirement releases the same pins as whole-instance retirement.

The existing startup and image-lock tests also check PCI retirement before
publishing a reusable instance state and before freeing Image segments.

Requests still in the shared ring
---------------------------------

Pinning a deferred worker cannot protect a request that has not reached its
handler. Generic parked confirmation therefore drains a captured prefix of the
local inbox after confirming every owned/tracked CPU stopped. With the controller
resource mutex held, the retiring peer can no longer publish requests or acquire
a replacement grant. The consumer lock also waits for any entered callback.
PCI requests reach the old grant before unlend revokes it; queued workers retain
that grant and cannot access or reply through a later one.

A claimed but unpublished slot returns -EBUSY, leaving the slot and the following
traffic intact. Callers retain the Image, grants and instance ID. If a live peer
publishes later, retry can succeed; an abandoned slot can block retirement until
controller reboot. The shared ring is never reset to recover one peer.

The captured prefix contains at most ring-size-minus-one entries, including
malformed ones. New traffic from other peers cannot extend this drain; it remains
queued for a later poll or doorbell. This bounds entries, not callback duration
or lock wait. Successful polling is not global ring quiescence. Deferred handlers
still need their own admission/identity and retirement guarantees.

Additional scenarios exercise old PCI requests before unlend/re-lend, unrelated
messages, unpublished slots, late publication, partial prefixes, wraparound,
continuous valid/malformed arrivals and callback reentry. cpu_ownership_test
checks that failed CPU confirmation prevents draining and a blocked drain cannot
report reusable confirmation.

Limits
------

These checks do not prove native lockdep behavior, PCI reset/DMA quiescence, or
RISC-V PCI assignment. Real cross-kernel producers, memory ordering, interrupt
delivery and CPU-stop proof need runtime qualification. A stopped peer must not
be able to publish via an untracked CPU or another kernel. Native device-remove
ACKs must mean the remote driver stopped issuing config requests. Delayed
resource-hotplug ACKs, partial overlay rollback and stale deferred non-PCI work
are separate lifetime gates; a passing fixture is not end-to-end safety.

Bounded deferred requests
-------------------------

Nested kernels use MK_IPI_RING_SIZE preinitialized system-workqueue slots.
A free slot has no device pin. Under mk_pciback_lock, admission takes the grant
reference and copies the request/subtype into a slot before queueing it. The
worker still serializes access and its ACK with revocation via the grant's
io_lock. It releases the grant before marking its slot free under the raw lock.
No request field is used afterward. Each work item keeps its callback and queue
and is never reinitialized in receive context, preserving workqueue
non-reentrancy if another request arrives before the old callback returns.
The slots have static lifetime and are initialized before the early PCI handler
registration. Registration errors are still propagated.

Full capacity drops the new request without taking a reference or sending a
success reply, matching the former allocation-failure behavior. The requester
can time out; no reset, grant reuse or recovery is implied. Direct config access
and negative replies remain synchronous because frontends may poll with
interrupts off. Process-context grant allocations and final reference release
remain; only IRQ-side work allocation/free/init is removed.

The handler copies the wire payload into aligned local storage before reading
its 64-bit sequence number or dispatching requests. The packed message layout
is unchanged. A deliberately misaligned ACK reproduces undefined behavior
before this copy; short messages are rejected as before.

The fixture now extracts the real payload/work definitions and early init,
checks allocation exhaustion without IRQ allocator entry, saturation without
overwrite or extra pins, copied request values, slot reuse, saturated revocation
and replacement-grant isolation, and unaligned request/ACK decoding. Existing
pthread retirement races and bounded ring-prefix tests remain. Run ASan/UBSan:

  CC=clang CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all' \
    bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/pciback_lifetime_test.sh

The scheduler remains controlled; PCI reset/DMA and actual nested-kernel config
forwarding are not qualified by the host fixture.
